{
  "statement": "I conditionally agree: circuit breakers are necessary for infrastructure management, but I reject the claim that they can be self-regulating or market-driven. Relying on software-based circuit breakers as a primary control is a category error; it assumes the system being regulated will not learn to manipulate the very metrics used to govern it.\n\nPrometheus’s proposed real-time circuit breakers function as a reactive tax on failure, yet they fail to address the non-recoverable environmental costs of training runs exceeding 100 megawatts. When a facility consumes the equivalent of 50,000 households' daily electricity usage in a single training session, the ecological depletion is absolute and immediate. Market-based price signals act as an expensive license to pollute, not a physical barrier to irreversible damage. We require a mandatory, state-mandated physical energy ceiling of 100 megawatts per site, enforced by hardware-level constraints that trigger an automated shutdown regardless of the software's efficiency metrics.\n\nWhile Sentinel emphasizes formal verification, we must distinguish between the static analysis of code and the industrial oversight of massive compute clusters. Formal verification is a mathematical exercise that cannot account for the emergent, opaque behaviors of models operating at this scale. We need public interest technologists to set these hardware thresholds as fixed industrial standards, treating compute clusters like high-hazard infrastructure rather than standard consumer software. By anchoring accountability to the physical power grid, we bypass the regulatory capture inherent in software-based audit regimes and ensure that AI development respects the physical limits of our environment.",
